<section class="section reveal" id="mlops-stack">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture</span>
  <h2>The MLOps Stack: Tools & Components</h2>

  <p>A modern MLOps platform requires multiple layers: data infrastructure, experiment tracking, model management, CI/CD automation, deployment orchestration, and monitoring. No single tool solves all problems — successful MLOps is about choosing the right tools and integrating them coherently.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Typical MLOps Architecture Stack</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Data Layer</h5>
        <p>Storage, versioning, lineage for training data and features.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>DVC</code> — data versioning</li>
          <li><code>Feast</code> — feature store</li>
          <li><code>Delta Lake</code> — ACID transactions</li>
          <li><code>Great Expectations</code> — validation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Experiment Layer</h5>
        <p>Track parameters, metrics, artifacts from training runs.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>MLflow</code> — tracking &amp; registry</li>
          <li><code>Weights &amp; Biases</code> — collaboration</li>
          <li><code>Neptune</code> — ML metadata</li>
          <li><code>Kubeflow</code> — orchestration</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Deployment Layer</h5>
        <p>Containerization, orchestration, serving infrastructure.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>Docker</code> — containerization</li>
          <li><code>Kubernetes</code> — orchestration</li>
          <li><code>BentoML</code> — serving</li>
          <li><code>Seldon</code> — deployment</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>Monitoring Layer</h5>
        <p>Track model performance, drift, system health in production.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>Prometheus</code> — metrics</li>
          <li><code>Grafana</code> — dashboards</li>
          <li><code>WhyLabs</code> — monitoring</li>
          <li><code>Arize</code> — observability</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Integration Patterns</h3>
  <p>Power emerges when layers communicate: data engineer loads new data (DVC tracked), data scientist experiments (MLflow tracked), validates via CI/CD, deploys via Kubernetes, monitors in Grafana. When drift is detected, the cycle repeats.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># MLflow integration for experiment tracking</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">mlflow</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="im">sklearn.ensemble</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="bi">RandomForestClassifier</span>

<span class="kw">with</span> <span class="fn">mlflow.start_run</span>():
    <span class="cm"># Log hyperparameters</span>
    <span class="fn">mlflow.log_params</span>({<span class="st">"n_estimators"</span>: <span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="st">"max_depth"</span>: <span class="nm">10</span>})

    <span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="bi">RandomForestClassifier</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>)
    <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">fit</span>(<span class="va">X_train</span>, <span class="va">y_train</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Log metrics</span>
    <span class="va">accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">score</span>(<span class="va">X_test</span>, <span class="va">y_test</span>)
    <span class="fn">mlflow.log_metric</span>(<span class="st">"accuracy"</span>, <span class="va">accuracy</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Log model artifact</span>
    <span class="fn">mlflow.sklearn.log_model</span>(<span class="va">model</span>, <span class="st">"model"</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> MLflow captures the entire experiment lifecycle. We log hyperparameters before training, metrics after training, and the model artifact itself. This creates a complete audit trail: trace any production model back to exactly which data, code version, and hyperparameters created it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Regulated Industries:</strong> Financial and healthcare organizations face strict compliance requirements. MLOps audit trails prove that models were trained on approved data, validated by appropriate stakeholders, and monitored for drift.</p>
    <p><strong>In Fast-Moving Teams:</strong> Without centralized tracking, models get lost, results aren't reproducible, and teams can't collaborate effectively. A unified MLOps platform is essential for team productivity.</p>
  </div>
</section>

