<section class="section reveal" id="challenges">
  <span class="section-label">Practical Insights</span>
  <h2>Common MLOps Challenges & Solutions</h2>

  <p>Building production ML systems is hard. Data quality issues that seem minor during development become critical in production. Models that work on Tuesday might fail on Wednesday due to data drift. Teams struggle with reproducibility: "I can't recreate the model I trained last month."</p>

  <h3>Challenge 1: Reproducibility</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Experiments fail to reproduce because of missing dependencies, different random seeds, or undocumented preprocessing.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Version everything: code (Git), data (DVC), environment (Docker), parameters (MLflow). Use deterministic seeds and document transformations in code.</p>

  <h3>Challenge 2: Data Quality & Drift</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Models fail when production data has missing values, outliers, or distribution shifts. You don't realize until users complain.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Implement data validation at every stage. Monitor incoming data distributions continuously. Set up automated alerts for drift and performance degradation.</p>

  <h3>Challenge 3: Model Governance</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Nobody knows who trained a model, when, with what data, or whether it passed validation. Multiple versions exist on different machines.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use a centralized model registry (MLflow, SageMaker). Tag models with metadata. Implement approval workflows before production deployment.</p>

  <h3>Challenge 4: Integration & Operationalization</h3>
  <p><strong>Problem:</strong> Data scientists develop in Jupyter notebooks. Moving to production requires manual rewriting by engineers, introducing bugs and delays.</p>
  <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use reproducible, framework-agnostic formats. Write models as pipelines with clear interfaces. Use orchestration (Airflow, Kubeflow) to automate workflows.</p>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">MLOps Best Practices Reference</div>
    <div class="stages">
      <div class="stage-card"><div class="stage-num">1</div><div class="stage-content"><h4>Version Everything</h4><p>Track code, data, models, configurations. Git for code, DVC for files, MLflow for parameters. Reproducibility depends on complete versioning.</p></div></div>
      <div class="stage-card"><div class="stage-num">2</div><div class="stage-content"><h4>Automate Testing</h4><p>Unit tests for data transformations, schema validation, model output bounds, integration tests for full pipeline. Catch bugs before production.</p></div></div>
      <div class="stage-card"><div class="stage-num">3</div><div class="stage-content"><h4>Monitor Everything</h4><p>Metrics (accuracy, latency), infrastructure (CPU, memory), data (drift, quality). Automated alerts trigger retraining or investigation before user impact.</p></div></div>
      <div class="stage-card"><div class="stage-num">4</div><div class="stage-content"><h4>Use Containers</h4><p>Docker ensures experiments run identically in development and production. No more "it works on my machine" problems. Essential for reproducibility and scaling.</p></div></div>
      <div class="stage-card"><div class="stage-num">5</div><div class="stage-content"><h4>Implement CI/CD</h4><p>Automated pipelines test code, validate data, train and evaluate models, deploy on success. Reduces manual errors and accelerates iteration cycles.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Reproducibility requires versioning code, data, environment, and parameters</li>
      <li>Data quality and drift are production's biggest challenges — monitor proactively</li>
      <li>Model governance ensures accountability and enables rollback if needed</li>
      <li>Automation bridges the gap between experimentation and production operations</li>
      <li>Culture matters: successful MLOps requires data and engineering team collaboration</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

