<section class="section reveal" id="getting-started">
  <span class="section-label">Next Steps</span>
  <h2>Building Your First MLOps Project</h2>

  <p>Starting with MLOps doesn't require massive infrastructure investment. Begin with core practices: version your code, track experiments, validate data, and containerize your model. As your system grows and organizational maturity increases, add orchestration, feature platforms, and advanced monitoring.</p>

  <h3>Minimal Viable MLOps Setup</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Git</strong> — version control for code and configurations</li>
    <li><strong>MLflow</strong> — experiment tracking and model registry</li>
    <li><strong>Docker</strong> — ensure reproducible environments</li>
    <li><strong>Great Expectations</strong> — validate data quality</li>
    <li><strong>Pytest</strong> — unit and integration tests</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub Actions or GitLab CI</strong> — simple CI/CD automation</li>
  </ul>

  <p>This minimal setup costs almost nothing but establishes reproducibility and governance. As team size and model complexity grow, add feature stores, distributed training, real-time serving, and sophisticated monitoring. The journey to mature MLOps is incremental, not a big bang.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>MLOps maturity grows incrementally — start with core practices</li>
      <li>Minimal viable setup: Git, MLflow, Docker, data validation, testing, CI/CD</li>
      <li>Scale complexity as organizational needs and team size grow</li>
      <li>Culture and processes matter as much as tools in MLOps adoption</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

